{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1368071-south-american-influence","id":1368071,"title":"South American Influence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-15T11:17:09-08:00","id":1368071,"title":"South American Influence","content":"Hi All - im running a new character through their awakening as a mage. The character is specifically a museum curator with a real love of language and culture of South/Central American native tribes, cultures and civilisations. He would really like his mage to have some link to that background. <br /> <br /> Any suggestions as to which Tradition or Craft might best fit this ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6295-dargurd","name":"Dargurd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-15T12:17:38-08:00","id":1368091,"title":"","content":"Hm. If we're talking here about someone who doesn't come from South/Central America themselves, one suggestion would be the Dreamspeakers, primarily either the Baruti (collectors and tellers of stories, particularly those of lost cultures) or the Keepers of the Sacred Flame (cultural preservers, mainly from within the cultures in question, but Western outsiders played an important role in establishing it as a group).<br /> <br /> There're other options across the Traditions which are generally focused on following various South/Central American practices, committing to a cultural practice; dunno if the character would want that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":51,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","user":"marin","date":". 02-15-2020, 03:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-15T12:59:49-08:00","id":1368100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>marin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1368071-south-american-influence?p=1368091#post1368091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> one suggestion would be the Dreamspeakers, primarily either the Baruti (collectors and tellers of stories, particularly those of lost cultures) or the Keepers of the Sacred Flame (cultural preservers, mainly from within the cultures in question, but Western outsiders played an important role in establishing it as a group).<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>This is perfect - thankyou. Just looked the Baruti up, they certainly sound interesting. <br /> <br /> What about perhaps The Order of Hermes. The character is very scholarly and i did wonder about that as an option.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6295-dargurd","name":"Dargurd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-15T14:12:54-08:00","id":1368127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dargurd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1368071-south-american-influence?p=1368100#post1368100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about perhaps The Order of Hermes. The character is very scholarly and i did wonder about that as an option.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mm. While academia's one of the Order's major recruiting pools, that doesn't mean it's necessarily a good fit; the Order tend to approach knowledge through the lens of how it can benefit Hermeticism and the Order, rather than as a way to understand the world or as a goal in itself. If he's okay with that, if he values the primacy of the Awakened Will, and/or he favors High Ritual and dominance in magickal practices, then I'd point to House Ex Miscellanea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-15T16:27:28-08:00","id":1368167,"title":"","content":"Random thoughts:<br /> <br /> There are a number of interesting museums (art and otherwise) throughout Latin America that could easily be the home base or favorite haunt of such a character. <i>El Museo del Oro</i> (The Museum of Gold) in Bogota, Colombia, has a massive collection of pre-Columbian gold artifacts, as well as other works by the native peoples of northern South America (the Muisca, Quimbaya, Zenu, and others). <i>Museu Nacional</i> (The National Museum of Brazil) in Rio de Janeiro has a large collection of South American artifacts, including a collection of Andean mummies, as well as ancient Egyptian and classical Mediterranean collections. They've also, naturally, got the largest collection of pre-colonial Brazilian artifacts in the world (Marajoara, Santarem, Tupi-Guarani, and others). <i>Museo Chileno de Arte Precolombino</i> (Chilean Museum of Pre-Columbian Art) in Santiago was originally someone's private collection until 1981, so a lot of its artifacts were originally collected for their looks rather than scientific value, but it has a very broad collection touching on at least a hundred different cultures over a period of several thousand years. <i>Museo Nacional de Antropologia</i> (National Museum of Anthropology) in Mexico City is one of that country's major museums, including a significant collection of Mayan, Aztec, and other native artifacts (including ones from the American Southwest and California), with it almost serving as a temple to Aztec culture.<br /> <br /> As far as Traditions go, everyone automatically goes to the <b>Dreamspeakers</b> when it comes to South America. And while they do have a sizable place there, including the <i>Keepers of the Sacred Fire</i> and the <i>Baruti</i>, both of which include their fair share of scholars, anthropologists, and linguists, they aren't the only game in town.<br /> <br /> The <b>Celestial Chorus</b>, with its religious overtones, has a fairly notable presence in South America. (I will note that the Chorus are technically Gnostics, but many of them tend to incorporate a lot of &quot;high church&quot;/Catholic ritual, imagery, and theurgy into their workings.) Their <i>Alexandrian Society</i> has a lot of interest in scholarly work, including trying to find traces of worship of The One in ancient cultures and preserving sacred relics. There is also an Incan faction of the Chorus mentioned in Guide to the Traditions (whose name I'm sadly blanking on at the moment) who joined during the Grand Convocation and have adapted to the modern world through a mix of synthesis with Catholicism and/or acting as a mystery cult that hides behind the mask of the Catholic Church in Peru. (There's also a small strain within the Chorus who preserve and cultivate sacred bloodlines, of which the Incan royal family could be one.)<br /> <br /> The <b>Order of Hermes</b> also has a place in South America. Obviously, they came over with Spanish and Portuguese colonial powers, but they've been there for a few hundred years. While <i>House Bonisagus</i> are usually the scholarly types, they tend to favor more abstract fields like physics or theurgy. But there are those that focus on language. However, when it comes to art, culture, and history, that's always been the forte of <i>House Jerbiton</i>. To them, the study of art and culture helps refine the soul, which in turn refines and strengthens their Art. (This has always made them a little odd by Hermetic standards, which is part of why they got stuffed into House Ex Miscellanea during the 1400s.) <i>House Ex Miscellanea</i> also has a number of mini-Houses who focus on things like synthesis of Hermetic Arts with other cultures. In my own games, I've used what I call <i>House Kukulkan</i> as a small group of mostly Mexican Hermetics who focus on learning all they can about the Mayans.<br /> <br /> South America also has a not insignificant Asian population, many of whom are third or forth generation, and have completely assimilated into modern Latin American culture. The <i>Li Hai</i> faction of the <b>Akashic Brotherhood</b> work to adapt the Akashic way to various different and modern cultures. One of their sub-factions, the <i>Rodo d'Oro</i> (Road of Gold), is largely based out of Brazil, heavily involved in Afro-Brazilian and Afro-Caribbean cultures (including the Candomble and Umbanda religions, and the Capoeira martial arts). The other two notable Li Hai factions, the <i>Blue Skins</i> (who embrace modern high fashion and worldly pleasures) and the <i>Mo-Tzu Fa</i> (folk heroes fighting for universal love and equality), both fit in really well with modern South American culture. It's also feasible for the benevolent aristocrats of the <i>Shi Ren</i> to have a place in modern South America, though they'd probably tend more toward being lawyers, politicians, and the like rather than cultural scholars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 02-15-2020, 07:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-15T22:21:02-08:00","id":1368224,"title":"","content":"Thankyou so much for the help and advice its really appreciated. Its also given me a lot of food for thought !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6295-dargurd","name":"Dargurd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}